// @check
// @error return type mismatch: expected i32, got string | i32
// THE EDGE OF `elseif-chain-residual.vl`'s WIDENING, pinned from the other side.
//
// A guard clause whose THEN diverges now post-narrows the fall-through even when the `if` has an
// `else` — but for BARE NAMES ONLY. This is the program that says why a property PATH may not
// join them.
//
// `b.v` is narrowed to `i32` on the false path of `b.v is string`. That narrowing rides the
// `npKeys` overlay, and `clobber(b)` INSIDE the else arm retires it (an aliasing write the
// checker can see — `callInvalidatesNarrowedPath`). But the overlay entry the else arm retired
// is popped with the else arm's own scope, so a post-`if` re-push would be a FRESH, un-retired
// entry: the narrowing comes back from the dead and `return b.v` reads a `string` through an
// `i32` view. That is not hypothetical — with paths included it compiled, and trapped:
//
//     wasm trap: cast failure
//     note: a value was not an instance of the type it was narrowed to.
//
// So the widening stops at paths, and the refusal below is the desired verdict. The NO-`else`
// spelling of the same hazard is not refused this way — it is caught by name, with a better
// message, because the retiring call is a statement of the enclosing block where the overlay
// entry is still live:
//
//     if b.v is string { return 0 }
//     clobber(b)
//     return b.v      // 'b.v' was narrowed by a guard, but the call to 'clobber' … can write it
//
// Both are refusals; only one can say why. Closing the gap properly means carrying the else
// arm's retirements out to the enclosing block rather than declining the whole channel.
type Box = { v: string | i32 }
function clobber(b: Box) { b.v = "surprise" }
function f(b: Box): i32 {
  if b.v is string { return 0 }
  else { clobber(b) }
  return b.v
}
print(f({ v: 3 }))
